                    {
                        "id":  "mass-combat-direct-involvement",
                        "title":  "Direct Involvement in Mass Combat",
                        "category":  "Warfare",
                        "summary":  "Individuals can alter large battles through frontline attacks, defense, sabotage, duels, spellcasting, and inspiration.",
                        "body":  [
                                     "Direct involvement treats heroes as concentrated influence within a much larger conflict. They may fight enemy formations, protect allied units, sabotage equipment, challenge leaders, or rally frightened troops.",
                                     "Their actions should change force strength, leadership, morale, or access to key battlefield assets rather than merely reproducing an isolated skirmish."
                                 ],
                        "sections":  [
                                         {
                                             "heading":  "Legacy Direct-Action Procedures",
                                             "sectionType":  "mechanics-legacy",
                                             "body":  [
                                                          "The source supplies attack rolls, skill checks, example casualty counts, unit health changes, and morale bonuses. These numerical procedures require conversion."
                                                      ]
                                         }
                                     ],
