                    {
                        "id":  "mass-combat-command-orders",
                        "title":  "Command and Orders in Mass Combat",
                        "category":  "Warfare",
                        "summary":  "Commanders shape battle through charges, defensive stands, flanking, retreat, fortification, dispersal, and ambush.",
                        "body":  [
                                     "Orders change how units apply their strength and accept risk. Aggressive commands can create breakthroughs while exposing troops; defensive commands preserve positions; movement orders trade concentrated power for position or coverage.",
                                     "Successful command depends on communicating intent and matching the order to terrain, morale, unit capability, and the enemy\u0027s likely response."
                                 ],
                        "sections":  [
                                         {
                                             "heading":  "Legacy Command Procedures",
                                             "sectionType":  "mechanics-legacy",
                                             "body":  [
                                                          "The source provides commanding rolls and numerical attack, defense, health, and advantage effects for seven orders. These procedures require conversion."
                                                      ]
                                         }
                                     ],
